  "claim": "Pinnacle Airlines Flight 3701 was headed to an airport when it crashed. That airport is 10 miles farther east than the airport that Jessica Dubroff took off from. Her plane crashed during a rainstorm.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Minneapolis–Saint Paul International Airport. Sentence: Minneapolis–Saint Paul International Airport (IATA: MSP, ICAO: KMSP, FAA LID: MSP) , also known as Wold–Chamberlain Field, is a joint civil-military public use international airport.\nPage: Cheyenne Regional Airport. Sentence: Cheyenne Regional Airport (IATA: CYS, ICAO: KCYS, FAA LID: CYS) (Jerry Olson Field) is a civil-military airport a mile north of downtown Cheyenne, in Laramie County, Wyoming.\nPage: Pinnacle Airlines Flight 3701. Sentence: It was an overnight repositioning flight with no passengers from Little Rock, Arkansas, U.S. to Minneapolis–Saint Paul International Airport, U.S. Both crew members were killed.\nPage: Jessica Dubroff. Sentence: On day-two of her quest, the Cessna 177B Cardinal single engine propeller aircraft, piloted by her flight instructor, crashed during a rainstorm immediately after takeoff from Cheyenne Regional Airport in Cheyenne, Wyoming, killing Dubroff, her 57-year-old father, and her flight instructor.",
